WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:05.306 --> 00:00:07.970
Hello, hello, hello, and welcome

00:00:07.970 --> 00:00:09.920
to English Learning for Curious

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:12.719
Minds, by Leonardo English, the show

00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:14.469
where you can listen to fascinating

00:00:14.469 --> 00:00:17.280
stories and learn weird and wonderful

00:00:17.290 --> 00:00:19.269
things about the world at the same

00:00:19.270 --> 00:00:20.960
time as improving your English.

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:24.340
I'm Alastair Budge, and today it's the

00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:27.300
start of another three-part mini-series,

00:00:27.559 --> 00:00:30.409
this time on famous con artists.

00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:33.340
In today's episode, we'll be meeting

00:00:33.379 --> 00:00:36.819
Victor Lustig, the smooth-talking "count"

00:00:37.119 --> 00:00:39.319
who managed to sell the Eiffel Tower, and

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:41.759
then came back to try to sell it again.

00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:44.040
In part two, we'll meet Gregor

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:46.500
MacGregor, the Scottish soldier

00:00:46.709 --> 00:00:49.040
who invented an entire country

00:00:49.239 --> 00:00:51.260
and sold it to the British public.

00:00:51.529 --> 00:00:54.069
And in part three, it's the story of

00:00:54.070 --> 00:00:56.690
Charles Ponzi, the Italian who ran a

00:00:56.699 --> 00:01:00.060
scheme so spectacular that his name

00:01:00.259 --> 00:01:02.479
became a word in the English language.

00:01:03.508 --> 00:01:05.668
OK then, let's not waste a

00:01:05.668 --> 00:01:08.308
minute and get right into it.

00:01:09.218 --> 00:01:12.138
It is a spring morning in 1925,

00:01:12.548 --> 00:01:15.238
and a man is having breakfast in a

00:01:15.248 --> 00:01:17.938
suite at the Hôtel de Crillon, one

00:01:17.938 --> 00:01:20.148
of the grandest hotels in Paris.

00:01:21.005 --> 00:01:22.848
He is in his mid-thirties.

00:01:23.098 --> 00:01:24.988
He is beautifully dressed, he

00:01:24.988 --> 00:01:27.727
speaks five languages, and has

00:01:27.727 --> 00:01:30.638
a long scar down his left cheek.

00:01:31.408 --> 00:01:32.928
He introduces himself to

00:01:32.978 --> 00:01:36.768
everyone as Count Victor Lustig.

00:01:37.858 --> 00:01:38.988
He is not a count.

00:01:39.308 --> 00:01:41.598
In fact, barely anything he

00:01:41.608 --> 00:01:43.628
says about himself is true.

00:01:44.798 --> 00:01:47.338
On this particular morning, he picks

00:01:47.338 --> 00:01:50.048
up a newspaper and starts to read

00:01:50.048 --> 00:01:52.228
an article about the Eiffel Tower.

00:01:53.448 --> 00:01:54.988
The tower, the article

00:01:54.998 --> 00:01:57.628
says, is in a sorry state.

00:01:58.208 --> 00:02:01.238
It is rusting, the paint is peeling,

00:02:01.608 --> 00:02:03.528
and it costs the French government

00:02:03.728 --> 00:02:06.528
a small fortune to maintain.

00:02:07.538 --> 00:02:09.398
The journalist even suggests,

00:02:09.778 --> 00:02:11.758
half-jokingly, that the people

00:02:11.788 --> 00:02:13.608
of Paris might be better

00:02:13.618 --> 00:02:15.718
off simply tearing it down.

00:02:17.068 --> 00:02:19.658
Lustig reads the article twice.

00:02:20.148 --> 00:02:21.758
Then he puts down the

00:02:21.758 --> 00:02:23.918
newspaper and smiles.

00:02:24.328 --> 00:02:26.388
He has just had the idea for

00:02:26.388 --> 00:02:29.068
one of the greatest con jobs

00:02:29.218 --> 00:02:30.728
of the twentieth century.

00:02:31.578 --> 00:02:34.368
This man, Victor Lustig, was born in

00:02:34.368 --> 00:02:38.538
1890, in a small town in Bohemia, in

00:02:38.688 --> 00:02:41.188
what is now the Czech Republic but was

00:02:41.188 --> 00:02:43.798
then part of the Austro-Hungarian Empire.

00:02:44.568 --> 00:02:47.248
His was a middle-class upbringing.

00:02:47.458 --> 00:02:50.618
His father was a tobacco salesman, and

00:02:50.858 --> 00:02:52.758
although he was abusive to his wife

00:02:52.777 --> 00:02:55.158
and children, he would take Victor with

00:02:55.158 --> 00:02:57.158
him on business trips around Europe.

00:02:57.768 --> 00:03:00.968
This, it would seem, gave the young boy

00:03:01.188 --> 00:03:04.428
an early insight into the riches of the

00:03:04.428 --> 00:03:07.138
great 19th century cities of Europe:

00:03:07.837 --> 00:03:10.958
Zurich, Prague, and of course, Paris.

00:03:11.898 --> 00:03:15.108
The boy, Victor, was clearly bright, but

00:03:15.528 --> 00:03:18.608
not particularly interested in hard work.

00:03:19.118 --> 00:03:21.078
There are reports of him being rude to

00:03:21.078 --> 00:03:23.728
teachers and running away from home, but

00:03:24.008 --> 00:03:26.868
still managing to get excellent grades.

00:03:27.818 --> 00:03:30.758
And like any sensible person who wants

00:03:30.758 --> 00:03:33.018
to seamlessly pass through different

00:03:33.058 --> 00:03:35.708
countries, he learned many languages.

00:03:36.058 --> 00:03:38.848
It's not clear quite how many, but

00:03:39.028 --> 00:03:41.068
it's thought that by the time he was

00:03:41.068 --> 00:03:43.698
an adult, he could move between Czech,

00:03:43.908 --> 00:03:46.798
German, English, French and Italian

00:03:46.978 --> 00:03:49.118
without any apparent effort — an

00:03:49.118 --> 00:03:51.148
important skill if you want to shift

00:03:51.168 --> 00:03:52.968
between different personalities.

00:03:54.171 --> 00:03:57.851
In 1909, he went to study at the Sorbonne

00:03:57.861 --> 00:04:00.351
in Paris, although it is unclear how

00:04:00.351 --> 00:04:02.481
much time he actually spent studying.

00:04:03.081 --> 00:04:05.681
What he did spend a lot of time doing

00:04:05.901 --> 00:04:09.331
was gambling, and it was here that he

00:04:09.381 --> 00:04:12.161
honed his skills at people-reading.

00:04:12.701 --> 00:04:15.540
Gambling, after all, is about figuring

00:04:15.591 --> 00:04:18.341
out your opponents — are they bluffing

00:04:18.701 --> 00:04:20.351
or are they telling the truth?

00:04:20.791 --> 00:04:22.881
And perhaps more importantly, can

00:04:22.891 --> 00:04:25.431
you trick them into believing you?

00:04:27.101 --> 00:04:28.871
As you might imagine, Lustig

00:04:28.911 --> 00:04:30.601
became very good at this.

00:04:31.231 --> 00:04:33.631
And knowing what he would get up to

00:04:33.961 --> 00:04:36.881
later in life, it seems highly probable

00:04:37.141 --> 00:04:38.991
that he would also have been cheating

00:04:38.991 --> 00:04:41.551
his way through these games, but he

00:04:41.551 --> 00:04:43.671
would no doubt have used his charisma

00:04:43.671 --> 00:04:46.540
and cunning to make his opponents think

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:48.191
they were just having a bad night.

00:04:49.071 --> 00:04:51.321
And, because this was early 20th

00:04:51.321 --> 00:04:54.111
century Paris, he also got into his

00:04:54.111 --> 00:04:57.571
fair share of romantic troubles, and the

00:04:57.571 --> 00:05:00.301
scar on his cheek was courtesy of the

00:05:00.381 --> 00:05:02.711
jealous boyfriend of a woman he'd been

00:05:02.711 --> 00:05:04.791
spending a little too much time with.

00:05:05.401 --> 00:05:07.901
So, here was a young man with

00:05:07.991 --> 00:05:12.451
charm, languages, quick wits, and no

00:05:12.491 --> 00:05:14.731
particular interest in honest work.

00:05:15.731 --> 00:05:18.691
He found his first hunting ground on

00:05:18.691 --> 00:05:21.081
the great ocean liners that crossed the

00:05:21.081 --> 00:05:24.051
Atlantic between Europe and New York.

00:05:24.981 --> 00:05:27.691
These ships were floating palaces,

00:05:27.981 --> 00:05:30.081
full of wealthy passengers with

00:05:30.091 --> 00:05:33.121
money to spend and time to kill.

00:05:33.981 --> 00:05:36.291
Lustig would travel first class,

00:05:36.491 --> 00:05:39.871
typically posing as a European aristocrat

00:05:39.951 --> 00:05:41.991
or a successful theatre producer.

00:05:42.611 --> 00:05:45.141
He would befriend rich passengers

00:05:45.271 --> 00:05:48.241
over dinner and cards, and tell them

00:05:48.421 --> 00:05:51.131
about some hit Broadway musical he was

00:05:51.131 --> 00:05:53.651
about to produce or some investment

00:05:53.681 --> 00:05:55.401
opportunity he had discovered.

00:05:56.111 --> 00:05:58.341
By the time the ship docked, they

00:05:58.341 --> 00:06:00.821
would practically beg him to let them

00:06:00.821 --> 00:06:03.201
invest in the project, a project which,

00:06:03.201 --> 00:06:05.371
of course, was entirely fictional.

00:06:05.541 --> 00:06:06.601
It did not exist.

00:06:07.981 --> 00:06:11.091
When the First World War put an end

00:06:11.241 --> 00:06:13.451
to the golden age of the ocean liner,

00:06:13.951 --> 00:06:16.631
Lustig moved to the United States,

00:06:16.841 --> 00:06:19.010
where he perfected the scheme he would

00:06:19.030 --> 00:06:21.821
become famous for: the money box.

00:06:22.781 --> 00:06:24.851
The money box was a small

00:06:24.881 --> 00:06:27.071
wooden chest, beautifully made,

00:06:27.391 --> 00:06:29.151
full of impressive-looking

00:06:29.151 --> 00:06:30.791
rollers and brass dials.

00:06:31.601 --> 00:06:33.871
Its special property, what made

00:06:33.871 --> 00:06:35.741
it magical, was that it could

00:06:35.841 --> 00:06:39.271
print perfect copies of banknotes.

00:06:40.470 --> 00:06:43.231
Lustig was always reluctant to talk

00:06:43.241 --> 00:06:45.651
about it, he said, and he only ever

00:06:45.651 --> 00:06:47.284
told people who could be trusted,

00:06:47.741 --> 00:06:49.620
who could be let in on the secret.

00:06:50.564 --> 00:06:53.100
He wouldemonstrate it in action.

00:06:53.567 --> 00:06:55.861
A realal hundred-dollar bill went

00:06:55.871 --> 00:06:58.001
in one slot, a piece of blank

00:06:58.001 --> 00:07:00.681
paper went in another, and the

00:07:00.681 --> 00:07:02.751
machine would begin its work.

00:07:03.621 --> 00:07:06.041
The only drawback, Lustig explained,

00:07:06.251 --> 00:07:08.321
was that the process was slow.

00:07:08.521 --> 00:07:10.201
It took six hours to

00:07:10.201 --> 00:07:11.821
produce a single note.

00:07:12.941 --> 00:07:16.181
And sure enough, six hours later, out

00:07:16.181 --> 00:07:19.491
came two identical hundred-dollar bills.

00:07:19.871 --> 00:07:22.341
They even appeared to have the same

00:07:22.381 --> 00:07:24.730
serial number, and they would be

00:07:24.790 --> 00:07:27.041
accepted by any bank in the country.

00:07:27.931 --> 00:07:29.851
And they were both real

00:07:30.121 --> 00:07:31.151
hundred-dollar bills.

00:07:31.671 --> 00:07:34.601
The "printed" bill was simply a

00:07:34.641 --> 00:07:37.018
genuine note that Lustig had hidden

00:07:37.018 --> 00:07:39.431
inside the machine beforehand, albeit

00:07:39.711 --> 00:07:41.711
with its serial number changed to

00:07:41.711 --> 00:07:43.581
match the one that would be "copied".

00:07:44.661 --> 00:07:46.761
Lustig would smile proudly,

00:07:46.941 --> 00:07:49.311
but would never explicitly

00:07:49.421 --> 00:07:50.631
offer to sell the machine.

00:07:51.061 --> 00:07:53.711
Of course, the victim would always offer.

00:07:54.091 --> 00:07:55.541
Lustig would refuse.

00:07:55.741 --> 00:07:57.051
The victim would offer more.

00:07:57.441 --> 00:08:00.271
Lustig would refuse again, then finally,

00:08:00.561 --> 00:08:02.981
as if he was doing them a great favour,

00:08:03.351 --> 00:08:05.641
agree to part with the wonderful

00:08:05.641 --> 00:08:07.721
machine for an appropriate price.

00:08:08.271 --> 00:08:10.041
Ten thousand dollars, sometimes

00:08:10.281 --> 00:08:11.941
double or triple that.

00:08:12.401 --> 00:08:15.331
One group of New York gamblers reportedly

00:08:15.371 --> 00:08:19.421
paid forty-six thousand, which is more

00:08:19.421 --> 00:08:23.241
than $700,000 in inflation-adjusted terms.

00:08:24.021 --> 00:08:26.441
The buyer would rush home with their

00:08:26.451 --> 00:08:28.961
prize, load it with paper, insert

00:08:28.961 --> 00:08:31.531
a hundred-dollar bill, and wait.

00:08:32.551 --> 00:08:34.441
Six hours later, out would

00:08:34.441 --> 00:08:36.451
come a real banknote, the last

00:08:36.451 --> 00:08:38.041
one Lustig had hidden inside.

00:08:38.701 --> 00:08:41.291
Then they would load it again, wait

00:08:41.341 --> 00:08:44.011
another six hours, and the machine

00:08:44.341 --> 00:08:46.851
would spit out a blank piece of paper.

00:08:48.411 --> 00:08:50.431
Understandably, they would try to find

00:08:50.441 --> 00:08:52.251
the man who had sold them the box.

00:08:52.821 --> 00:08:53.731
Could he help?

00:08:53.931 --> 00:08:55.141
Had something gone wrong,

00:08:55.721 --> 00:08:56.551
or had he tricked them?

00:08:57.831 --> 00:08:59.591
By that time, Lustig would

00:08:59.601 --> 00:09:02.001
be hundreds of miles away, in

00:09:02.001 --> 00:09:04.501
another city, under another name.

00:09:05.241 --> 00:09:07.051
And there were plenty of names.

00:09:07.721 --> 00:09:10.891
Over his career, Lustig used dozens of

00:09:10.901 --> 00:09:13.371
false identities, complete with matching

00:09:13.381 --> 00:09:16.281
documents: Robert Duval, Robert Miller,

00:09:16.581 --> 00:09:18.951
and more than one invented count.

00:09:19.631 --> 00:09:22.061
By the end, the file the American

00:09:22.091 --> 00:09:24.111
authorities kept on him listed

00:09:24.121 --> 00:09:26.705
forty-seven different aliases.

00:09:27.651 --> 00:09:30.151
Now, the money box was a clever

00:09:30.151 --> 00:09:32.491
trick, and the six hour delay

00:09:32.641 --> 00:09:34.261
made the entire thing work.

00:09:34.521 --> 00:09:37.641
But there was a second layer of

00:09:37.641 --> 00:09:39.931
protection, and that was that his

00:09:39.931 --> 00:09:43.031
victims almost never went to the police.

00:09:43.831 --> 00:09:45.711
After all, printing fake

00:09:45.781 --> 00:09:47.401
currency was a crime.

00:09:47.821 --> 00:09:49.261
They were hardly likely to go to the

00:09:49.261 --> 00:09:51.321
authorities and say that they had been

00:09:51.351 --> 00:09:53.621
planning to commit a felony, a serious

00:09:53.631 --> 00:09:56.161
crime, but had been tricked by a criminal.

00:09:57.051 --> 00:10:00.221
However, this didn't work every time.

00:10:00.861 --> 00:10:03.661
One of his money box victims was a

00:10:03.661 --> 00:10:06.451
real Texas sheriff, who handed over

00:10:06.601 --> 00:10:08.831
thousands of dollars for the machine.

00:10:10.001 --> 00:10:12.961
Now, why a real sheriff would be

00:10:12.961 --> 00:10:15.481
buying a money-printing box is another

00:10:15.481 --> 00:10:18.241
question altogether, and one you can

00:10:18.311 --> 00:10:20.821
perhaps answer by accepting that law

00:10:20.851 --> 00:10:23.011
officials back then weren't always

00:10:23.021 --> 00:10:25.231
more law-abiding than regular citizens.

00:10:26.061 --> 00:10:29.151
In any case, when the sheriff worked

00:10:29.151 --> 00:10:31.841
out that he had been swindled, he

00:10:31.881 --> 00:10:34.001
tracked Lustig across the country,

00:10:34.381 --> 00:10:36.461
all the way to Chicago, and

00:10:36.481 --> 00:10:40.001
confronted him, furious and armed.

00:10:40.981 --> 00:10:43.041
Lustig remained calm.

00:10:43.551 --> 00:10:45.761
Perhaps the smooth-talking young man

00:10:45.981 --> 00:10:49.041
had prepared for this very eventuality.

00:10:49.997 --> 00:10:53.846
He listened patiently, frowned,  inspected

00:10:53.846 --> 00:10:56.326
the machine, and explained that it was in

00:10:56.386 --> 00:10:58.776
perfect working order; the sheriff must

00:10:58.786 --> 00:11:01.426
simply have been operating it incorrectly.

00:11:02.356 --> 00:11:03.716
He went through the instructions

00:11:03.716 --> 00:11:06.556
again, step by step, got the machine

00:11:06.596 --> 00:11:09.486
back into working order, apologised

00:11:09.566 --> 00:11:12.316
sincerely for the confusion, and by

00:11:12.316 --> 00:11:15.026
way of apology, handed the sheriff a

00:11:15.066 --> 00:11:17.586
thick envelope of cash for his trouble.

00:11:18.496 --> 00:11:20.686
The sheriff went home satisfied.

00:11:21.046 --> 00:11:23.066
What excellent customer service!

00:11:23.866 --> 00:11:25.986
His mind was changed, however, when

00:11:25.986 --> 00:11:27.816
he tried to pay for something with

00:11:27.816 --> 00:11:29.606
one of the banknotes Lustig had

00:11:29.616 --> 00:11:32.356
given him and ended up being arrested

00:11:32.546 --> 00:11:34.256
for having counterfeit currency.

00:11:34.896 --> 00:11:37.156
The compensation money was fake.

00:11:37.486 --> 00:11:39.036
Lustig had managed to

00:11:39.106 --> 00:11:41.466
con the same man twice.

00:11:42.266 --> 00:11:46.015
Now, back to Paris, in May 1925,

00:11:46.146 --> 00:11:47.736
and that newspaper article.

00:11:48.806 --> 00:11:51.916
To understand what Lustig did next,

00:11:52.216 --> 00:11:53.996
you need to know that the Eiffel

00:11:53.996 --> 00:11:56.906
Tower was never meant to be permanent.

00:11:57.716 --> 00:12:01.016
It had been built in 1889 as the

00:12:01.036 --> 00:12:03.726
entrance to the World's Fair and

00:12:03.926 --> 00:12:05.856
the original permission was for

00:12:05.856 --> 00:12:07.946
it to stand for just twenty years.

00:12:09.036 --> 00:12:11.166
Many Parisians hated it.

00:12:11.816 --> 00:12:14.256
Some of France's most famous artists

00:12:14.466 --> 00:12:16.936
had signed a public protest against it,

00:12:17.356 --> 00:12:20.136
comparing it to a gigantic black factory

00:12:20.146 --> 00:12:22.696
chimney looming over their beautiful city.

00:12:23.886 --> 00:12:26.236
The famous author Guy de Maupassant

00:12:26.706 --> 00:12:29.506
even famously said he ate lunch at the

00:12:29.516 --> 00:12:31.636
Tower's restaurant every day because

00:12:31.636 --> 00:12:33.666
it was the only place in Paris where

00:12:33.666 --> 00:12:35.286
you couldn't see the wretched thing.

00:12:36.676 --> 00:12:40.056
By 1925, it was thirty-six years

00:12:40.056 --> 00:12:42.856
old, rusting, and not the sort

00:12:42.856 --> 00:12:44.786
of place American tourists would

00:12:44.796 --> 00:12:46.636
propose under as they do today.

00:12:47.340 --> 00:12:50.685
So when a newspaper article suggested

00:12:50.869 --> 00:12:53.586
it might one day be dismantled, this

00:12:53.586 --> 00:12:55.756
was not a completely absurd idea.

00:12:57.140 --> 00:12:59.556
Lustig's plan was built on that

00:12:59.556 --> 00:13:01.956
small foundation of plausibility.

00:13:02.996 --> 00:13:06.276
First, he paid a skilled forger to

00:13:06.276 --> 00:13:08.496
produce official government stationery

00:13:08.496 --> 00:13:11.166
for him, complete with the seal of the

00:13:11.176 --> 00:13:13.246
ministry responsible for the tower.

00:13:14.196 --> 00:13:17.966
Then he wrote to six of the biggest scrap

00:13:18.026 --> 00:13:20.676
metal dealers in Paris, on that official

00:13:20.676 --> 00:13:23.656
paper, inviting them to a confidential

00:13:23.656 --> 00:13:25.906
meeting at the Hôtel de Crillon.

00:13:27.136 --> 00:13:28.426
All six came.

00:13:28.946 --> 00:13:29.796
Of course they came.

00:13:29.956 --> 00:13:32.196
I mean, a letter from a senior government

00:13:32.206 --> 00:13:35.246
official, on official paper, summoning

00:13:35.256 --> 00:13:37.646
them to one of the finest hotels in Paris?

00:13:38.256 --> 00:13:39.076
This wasn't something

00:13:39.076 --> 00:13:40.316
you'd turn your nose up at.

00:13:41.186 --> 00:13:43.706
And Lustig was relying on a rule

00:13:43.976 --> 00:13:45.526
that had served him his whole

00:13:45.526 --> 00:13:48.496
career: the bigger and bolder the

00:13:48.496 --> 00:13:51.216
lie, the fewer questions people ask.

00:13:51.956 --> 00:13:55.126
A man selling gold watches in a smoky

00:13:55.126 --> 00:13:57.736
bar expects some level of suspicion.

00:13:58.176 --> 00:14:00.996
But who on earth would have the nerve

00:14:01.256 --> 00:14:03.306
to invent the sale of the Eiffel Tower?

00:14:03.726 --> 00:14:05.886
The sheer size of the claim

00:14:06.036 --> 00:14:07.336
was its own protection.

00:14:08.956 --> 00:14:11.306
Lustig received them in his suite

00:14:11.566 --> 00:14:14.306
and introduced himself as the Deputy

00:14:14.356 --> 00:14:16.006
Director-General of the ministry.

00:14:16.686 --> 00:14:18.286
He apologised for the secrecy,

00:14:18.766 --> 00:14:20.836
and then he delivered the pitch.

00:14:21.466 --> 00:14:23.236
The government, he explained,

00:14:23.646 --> 00:14:25.216
had made a difficult decision.

00:14:25.756 --> 00:14:27.536
The Eiffel Tower was simply

00:14:27.586 --> 00:14:30.136
too expensive to maintain, and

00:14:30.266 --> 00:14:32.616
it was to be sold for scrap.

00:14:33.296 --> 00:14:35.806
Seven thousand tonnes of iron, gentlemen.

00:14:36.566 --> 00:14:38.476
The state required a discreet

00:14:38.516 --> 00:14:41.146
buyer, because if the news got out

00:14:41.216 --> 00:14:42.686
before the contracts were signed,

00:14:43.036 --> 00:14:45.126
there would be a public outcry.

00:14:45.936 --> 00:14:47.826
He even took them to the tower

00:14:47.826 --> 00:14:49.996
itself, in a rented limousine,

00:14:50.186 --> 00:14:52.176
and walked them around it like an

00:14:52.176 --> 00:14:54.296
estate agent showing off a house.

00:14:54.986 --> 00:14:56.886
Of course, it's not like the metal

00:14:56.886 --> 00:14:58.566
dealers didn't know what it was,

00:14:58.746 --> 00:15:01.576
but this all added to the spectacle.

00:15:02.406 --> 00:15:03.946
If you are interested, gentlemen,

00:15:04.176 --> 00:15:06.426
please submit your offer, what you

00:15:06.426 --> 00:15:07.906
are willing to pay for the metal.

00:15:08.926 --> 00:15:11.246
The dealers submitted their bids.

00:15:11.456 --> 00:15:14.886
But Lustig had already chosen his winner.

00:15:16.036 --> 00:15:18.076
His name was André Poisson.

00:15:19.196 --> 00:15:21.216
He was newer to the business than

00:15:21.216 --> 00:15:22.776
the others, less established,

00:15:23.036 --> 00:15:25.526
and desperate to break into the

00:15:25.586 --> 00:15:27.936
inner circle of Parisian commerce.

00:15:28.516 --> 00:15:30.936
Lustig had watched him during

00:15:30.936 --> 00:15:33.996
the meeting and seen exactly what

00:15:33.996 --> 00:15:36.446
he needed to see: desperation.

00:15:37.296 --> 00:15:39.606
Poisson needed this more

00:15:39.606 --> 00:15:40.906
than any of the others.

00:15:42.126 --> 00:15:43.776
Poisson's wife, to her

00:15:43.776 --> 00:15:45.676
credit, smelled a rat.

00:15:46.286 --> 00:15:46.996
She thought the whole

00:15:47.006 --> 00:15:48.336
thing seemed suspicious.

00:15:48.826 --> 00:15:50.116
Why the secrecy?

00:15:50.426 --> 00:15:52.776
Why the hotel suite instead

00:15:52.786 --> 00:15:53.866
of a government office?

00:15:54.893 --> 00:15:57.533
Lustig seemed to anticipate this

00:15:57.533 --> 00:15:59.533
scepticism, and approached it,

00:15:59.923 --> 00:16:01.753
I have to say, quite cleverly.

00:16:02.773 --> 00:16:04.883
He called Poisson to a private

00:16:04.883 --> 00:16:07.163
meeting, and he confessed.

00:16:07.793 --> 00:16:10.703
Yes, there was something else going on.

00:16:11.713 --> 00:16:13.913
The truth was, he said, that he

00:16:13.913 --> 00:16:15.683
was a government official with a

00:16:15.683 --> 00:16:17.473
mere government official's salary.

00:16:17.933 --> 00:16:20.213
Men in his position were expected

00:16:20.213 --> 00:16:22.503
to entertain, to dress well,

00:16:23.013 --> 00:16:24.603
to live a certain kind of life.

00:16:25.493 --> 00:16:27.983
He was, he admitted with some

00:16:28.013 --> 00:16:30.503
embarrassment, looking for a little

00:16:30.503 --> 00:16:33.433
something extra for himself, a sweetener,

00:16:33.923 --> 00:16:35.423
and then the deal would be Poisson's.

00:16:36.023 --> 00:16:38.223
He was asking for a bribe.

00:16:39.073 --> 00:16:42.163
And this, strangely enough, is what

00:16:42.163 --> 00:16:43.843
seems to have convinced Poisson.

00:16:44.903 --> 00:16:47.983
A fraudster might fake a ministry letter.

00:16:48.163 --> 00:16:51.413
But surely only a real, flesh-and-blood

00:16:51.463 --> 00:16:53.823
French official would ask for a bribe.

00:16:55.343 --> 00:16:57.553
Poisson handed over the money for

00:16:57.553 --> 00:17:00.363
the tower, plus the bribe on top.

00:17:01.353 --> 00:17:03.883
It's unclear exactly how much this

00:17:03.883 --> 00:17:06.203
was, for reasons I'll explain in a

00:17:06.203 --> 00:17:09.043
minute, but it was a small fortune.

00:17:09.653 --> 00:17:11.873
I mean, you don't have to be a scrap

00:17:11.893 --> 00:17:15.153
metal expert to know that 7,000 tonnes

00:17:15.153 --> 00:17:17.402
of iron would be worth quite a lot.

00:17:18.823 --> 00:17:21.713
Lustig was on a train to Austria within

00:17:21.713 --> 00:17:24.703
hours, carrying a suitcase full of cash.

00:17:25.573 --> 00:17:27.953
And then he did something remarkable.

00:17:28.583 --> 00:17:30.833
He sat in Vienna and read

00:17:31.063 --> 00:17:32.553
the French newspapers, every

00:17:32.553 --> 00:17:34.183
day, looking for the story.

00:17:35.403 --> 00:17:36.333
It never appeared.

00:17:37.383 --> 00:17:40.233
Lustig had calculated, correctly,

00:17:40.593 --> 00:17:42.133
that Poisson would be too

00:17:42.163 --> 00:17:43.693
ashamed to go to the police.

00:17:44.443 --> 00:17:47.303
To report the crime would be to announce

00:17:47.303 --> 00:17:49.253
to all of Paris that he had been

00:17:49.283 --> 00:17:51.863
duped in the most outrageous of ways.

00:17:52.263 --> 00:17:54.833
His reputation, the very thing

00:17:54.843 --> 00:17:56.323
he had been trying to establish,

00:17:56.643 --> 00:17:59.043
would be destroyed in an instant.

00:18:00.083 --> 00:18:02.243
The silence told Lustig something

00:18:02.243 --> 00:18:05.923
else, too: the scam was reusable.

00:18:06.523 --> 00:18:09.553
So a few months later, he came back to

00:18:09.553 --> 00:18:12.363
Paris and ran the entire scheme again,

00:18:12.723 --> 00:18:15.333
with a fresh set of scrap metal dealers.

00:18:16.043 --> 00:18:18.083
This time, though, one of them went to

00:18:18.083 --> 00:18:20.303
the police, and when Lustig got wind

00:18:20.443 --> 00:18:22.133
that the authorities were onto him,

00:18:22.333 --> 00:18:24.853
he fled on the first ship to America.

00:18:26.013 --> 00:18:28.223
Remarkably, he was never convicted

00:18:28.223 --> 00:18:30.543
for it, and as far as the records

00:18:30.543 --> 00:18:33.023
show, he was never even charged.

00:18:33.693 --> 00:18:36.633
Poisson stayed silent, and by

00:18:36.633 --> 00:18:38.413
the time anyone else spoke up,

00:18:38.723 --> 00:18:40.953
Lustig was on another continent.

00:18:41.643 --> 00:18:44.203
The full story only came out years

00:18:44.203 --> 00:18:46.123
later, from the victims themselves,

00:18:46.343 --> 00:18:48.253
once the embarrassment had faded.

00:18:49.293 --> 00:18:52.013
Back in America, Lustig continued his

00:18:52.013 --> 00:18:54.663
life as a professional con man, selling

00:18:54.663 --> 00:18:57.033
more money boxes, counterfeiting currency,

00:18:57.373 --> 00:18:59.323
and generally getting up to no good.

00:19:00.103 --> 00:19:02.023
He even, at one point in the

00:19:02.023 --> 00:19:04.453
Great Depression, managed to con

00:19:04.453 --> 00:19:06.843
the legendary mobster Al Capone.

00:19:07.553 --> 00:19:08.833
The story goes like this.

00:19:09.483 --> 00:19:13.103
Lustig went to Capone and proposed a deal.

00:19:13.883 --> 00:19:16.043
Give me fifty thousand dollars, he

00:19:16.043 --> 00:19:19.123
said, and I will double it in two

00:19:19.123 --> 00:19:21.663
months through a stock market operation.

00:19:22.743 --> 00:19:24.773
Capone handed over the money,

00:19:25.013 --> 00:19:26.933
with the clear understanding that

00:19:26.933 --> 00:19:28.573
something very unpleasant would

00:19:28.583 --> 00:19:30.713
happen to Lustig if it disappeared.

00:19:31.773 --> 00:19:33.983
Lustig took the fifty thousand

00:19:33.983 --> 00:19:36.973
dollars, put it in a safe deposit box,

00:19:37.453 --> 00:19:39.503
and did absolutely nothing with it.

00:19:40.433 --> 00:19:42.553
Two months later, he returned to

00:19:42.553 --> 00:19:45.213
Capone, wearing his best suit and

00:19:45.213 --> 00:19:47.173
his most apologetic expression.

00:19:48.063 --> 00:19:50.733
The deal had fallen through, he said.

00:19:50.913 --> 00:19:52.373
He was terribly sorry.

00:19:52.573 --> 00:19:53.943
But he would reimburse

00:19:53.943 --> 00:19:55.983
Capone out of his own funds.

00:19:56.363 --> 00:19:58.813
And he handed back the fifty thousand

00:19:58.823 --> 00:20:01.023
dollars, every single note of it.

00:20:02.043 --> 00:20:03.913
Capone was astonished.

00:20:04.513 --> 00:20:07.183
He dealt with liars and thieves all day

00:20:07.183 --> 00:20:10.123
long, and here was a man returning money

00:20:10.252 --> 00:20:12.333
he could very easily have run away with.

00:20:13.053 --> 00:20:15.223
He was so impressed by this display

00:20:15.223 --> 00:20:17.273
of honesty that he peeled off

00:20:17.283 --> 00:20:19.333
five thousand dollars and gave

00:20:19.333 --> 00:20:20.833
it to Lustig for his trouble.

00:20:21.313 --> 00:20:23.403
Which, of course, is exactly what Lustig

00:20:23.403 --> 00:20:25.063
had wanted from the very beginning.

00:20:26.133 --> 00:20:28.279
Now, the law did eventually

00:20:28.323 --> 00:20:29.303
catch up with Lustig.

00:20:29.803 --> 00:20:31.763
Or rather, a jilted lover did.

00:20:32.663 --> 00:20:36.193
In 1935, his mistress discovered

00:20:36.333 --> 00:20:38.673
that he was seeing another woman, and

00:20:38.833 --> 00:20:40.593
she made an anonymous phone call to

00:20:40.593 --> 00:20:43.063
the authorities, informing on a fake

00:20:43.133 --> 00:20:45.103
currency business Lustig was running.

00:20:46.003 --> 00:20:48.103
When agents stopped him on a New York

00:20:48.103 --> 00:20:50.963
street, Lustig was as smooth as ever.

00:20:51.513 --> 00:20:53.723
But he had a key in his pocket

00:20:54.023 --> 00:20:55.353
that he could not explain.

00:20:56.043 --> 00:20:59.053
The key led to a locker in Times

00:20:59.063 --> 00:21:01.863
Square subway station, and the locker

00:21:02.203 --> 00:21:04.553
contained fifty-one thousand dollars

00:21:04.763 --> 00:21:06.813
in counterfeit notes, along with the

00:21:06.813 --> 00:21:08.573
printing plates used to make them.

00:21:09.573 --> 00:21:10.913
The game was up.

00:21:11.490 --> 00:21:13.873
But even then, he had one

00:21:13.913 --> 00:21:15.243
last performance in him.

00:21:15.943 --> 00:21:18.133
The day before his trial, Lustig

00:21:18.383 --> 00:21:20.823
escaped from jail in New York.

00:21:21.463 --> 00:21:23.753
He had made a rope from bedsheets,

00:21:23.923 --> 00:21:26.023
and he lowered himself down the side

00:21:26.023 --> 00:21:28.083
of the building in broad daylight.

00:21:28.993 --> 00:21:31.453
When people on the street below stopped

00:21:31.493 --> 00:21:34.293
and stared, he calmly took out a cloth

00:21:34.493 --> 00:21:36.653
and began wiping the windows as he went

00:21:36.653 --> 00:21:39.133
down, floor by floor, like a window

00:21:39.133 --> 00:21:41.033
cleaner going about his business.

00:21:42.103 --> 00:21:44.013
At the bottom, he bowed to

00:21:44.013 --> 00:21:45.853
his audience, and vanished.

00:21:47.303 --> 00:21:49.903
He was, however, recaptured in

00:21:49.933 --> 00:21:51.933
Pittsburgh almost a month later.

00:21:52.503 --> 00:21:53.913
This time there would be

00:21:53.913 --> 00:21:55.433
no talking his way out.

00:21:56.073 --> 00:21:58.223
He pleaded guilty and was sentenced

00:21:58.233 --> 00:22:00.483
to twenty years, to be served in

00:22:00.483 --> 00:22:02.993
the new maximum-security prison on a

00:22:02.993 --> 00:22:06.043
rock in San Francisco Bay: Alcatraz.

00:22:07.033 --> 00:22:09.693
Lustig spent more than a decade there.

00:22:10.213 --> 00:22:12.983
In March 1947, he developed

00:22:13.013 --> 00:22:14.903
pneumonia, and he died in a

00:22:14.933 --> 00:22:16.813
prison hospital in Springfield,

00:22:16.813 --> 00:22:19.053
Missouri, at the age of fifty-seven.

00:22:20.293 --> 00:22:21.813
On his death certificate,

00:22:22.003 --> 00:22:24.423
under "occupation" a clerk

00:22:24.423 --> 00:22:26.273
wrote "apprentice salesman".

00:22:27.293 --> 00:22:29.563
His sales might have been utterly

00:22:29.573 --> 00:22:32.633
dishonest, but it seems a little unfair

00:22:32.743 --> 00:22:34.753
to classify them as "apprentice".

00:22:35.363 --> 00:22:37.493
After all, if you can sell something

00:22:37.503 --> 00:22:40.363
you do not own, and that thing is the

00:22:40.403 --> 00:22:43.743
Eiffel Tower, and you almost manage

00:22:43.743 --> 00:22:46.533
to sell it twice, then you might be

00:22:46.533 --> 00:22:48.343
the greatest salesman in the world.

00:22:49.803 --> 00:22:52.263
OK, then, that is it for today's

00:22:52.263 --> 00:22:53.933
episode — the first part of our

00:22:53.963 --> 00:22:56.743
three-part series on famous con artists.

00:22:57.263 --> 00:22:59.543
Next up we'll be moving into the story of

00:22:59.553 --> 00:23:01.833
Gregor MacGregor, the Scottish soldier who

00:23:02.133 --> 00:23:03.883
went one better than selling a monument,

00:23:04.183 --> 00:23:06.673
and invented an entire country, complete

00:23:06.673 --> 00:23:09.323
with settlers, bonds and its own currency.

00:23:09.633 --> 00:23:11.393
And in part three, we'll follow

00:23:11.443 --> 00:23:13.853
Charles Ponzi to Boston in the summer

00:23:13.873 --> 00:23:16.633
of 1920, to see how one man took

00:23:16.663 --> 00:23:18.703
in a million dollars a day, and how

00:23:18.703 --> 00:23:20.673
his name ended up in a dictionary.

00:23:22.103 --> 00:23:23.933
You've been listening to English Learning

00:23:23.933 --> 00:23:26.643
for Curious Minds by Leonardo English.

00:23:26.993 --> 00:23:29.963
I'm Alastair Budge, you stay safe, and

00:23:29.963 --> 00:23:31.793
I'll catch you in the next episode.

